WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.586 --> 00:00:03.140
(swooping music)

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:04.501
- Good morning, Believe Nation.

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:05.334
It's Evan.

00:00:05.334 --> 00:00:06.599
My one word is believe,

00:00:06.599 --> 00:00:08.805
and I believe that you
have a special ability

00:00:08.805 --> 00:00:11.880
to create something amazing
that can change the planet.

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:12.960
So to help you in your journey,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.920
today we're going to learn how
to communicate effectively.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.503
(upbeat music)

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:33.484
(tingly music)

00:00:33.484 --> 00:00:36.331
(upbeat music)

00:00:36.331 --> 00:00:40.412
- This is going to blow away
people especially your fan base.

00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:41.522
Listening.

00:00:41.522 --> 00:00:44.570
You know, I speak a lot,
and I talk over people,

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.519
and I'm loud and intense

00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:50.408
but all of my success has
been predicated on listening

00:00:50.408 --> 00:00:53.902
whether I'm doing it with
my ears or with my eyes.

00:00:53.902 --> 00:00:57.118
- Skill I use is probably communication,

00:00:57.118 --> 00:00:59.074
which I think is building
on what Gary said.

00:00:59.074 --> 00:01:02.092
I think communication is a two way street,

00:01:02.092 --> 00:01:04.535
but being able to
articulate and communicate

00:01:04.535 --> 00:01:06.799
what it is that I'm trying to communicate

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:10.507
is something that I find
I focus on all day long,

00:01:10.507 --> 00:01:12.134
every time I speak.

00:01:12.134 --> 00:01:13.974
- It turns out in corporate America today,

00:01:13.974 --> 00:01:15.448
and I teach this now,

00:01:15.448 --> 00:01:18.182
I taught at Notre Dame and
MIT to graduating cohorts,

00:01:18.182 --> 00:01:19.940
I explain this to them,

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.554
when you're leading, when
you want to be a leader,

00:01:21.554 --> 00:01:24.212
you have to articulate your
idea in 90 seconds or less.

00:01:24.212 --> 00:01:26.893
You have to explain why you're
the right person to lead,

00:01:26.893 --> 00:01:29.720
and above all you have
to know your numbers.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.455
You can see it happen on Shark Tank,

00:01:31.455 --> 00:01:33.887
it works in the real world, too.

00:01:33.887 --> 00:01:35.919
And frankly, Shark Tank is the real world,

00:01:35.919 --> 00:01:37.422
'cause it's real capital.

00:01:37.422 --> 00:01:39.170
So I use that and I teach it

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.068
and I think everybody
should think about that,

00:01:41.068 --> 00:01:44.643
explaining your vision
clearly, succinctly,

00:01:44.643 --> 00:01:48.474
in a short period of time is
something really important.

00:01:48.474 --> 00:01:51.641
(inspirational music)

00:01:53.204 --> 00:01:54.776
- Before a conversation,

00:01:54.776 --> 00:01:57.399
put your mind in the other person's body,

00:01:57.399 --> 00:02:00.081
and think what's good for them, right?

00:02:00.081 --> 00:02:02.205
Because you don't just want
to be this selfish bulldog

00:02:02.205 --> 00:02:03.389
that's running through life

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:04.782
and not thinking about other people,

00:02:04.782 --> 00:02:07.591
and also a conversation
is going to go better

00:02:07.591 --> 00:02:09.599
if you think about what is going to help

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:12.194
this person achieve their goals,

00:02:12.194 --> 00:02:14.132
what motivates them,
what are their values.

00:02:14.132 --> 00:02:16.860
The best story I've ever
heard for this is Charlie.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.949
Charlie wanted to move to New York City,

00:02:18.949 --> 00:02:20.580
but his job was in Washington D.C.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:21.660
He could've just quit,

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:22.925
he could've just put in
his two weeks notice,

00:02:22.925 --> 00:02:24.098
he could've done it via email,

00:02:24.098 --> 00:02:26.553
'cause he was really scared
to have this conversation,

00:02:26.553 --> 00:02:29.199
but instead he got some
time with his boss,

00:02:29.199 --> 00:02:31.184
sat down and said,

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:33.599
"Hey, I really like it here,
I really like the people,

00:02:33.599 --> 00:02:36.321
"but I'm unhappy in D.C.

00:02:36.321 --> 00:02:37.934
"All my friends are in New York,

00:02:37.934 --> 00:02:39.130
"my best friend's in New York,

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:41.030
"it's important to me to be happy,

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:42.615
"I need to move.

00:02:42.615 --> 00:02:45.871
"But I want to do this in the
best way possible for you,

00:02:45.871 --> 00:02:48.656
"so does that mean working remotely,

00:02:48.656 --> 00:02:51.123
"does that mean you
guys hire my replacement

00:02:51.123 --> 00:02:52.963
"and I train them and
it takes a couple months

00:02:52.963 --> 00:02:55.668
"for me to go get my ideal outcome,

00:02:55.668 --> 00:02:56.863
"which is to go to New York.

00:02:56.863 --> 00:03:00.613
"How can I do this so
it's good for both of us?"

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:01.948
And they had a great conversation,

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:04.159
ended up with Charlie working remotely,

00:03:04.159 --> 00:03:06.115
first person of his position to do it,

00:03:06.115 --> 00:03:07.467
and he got a 50% raise

00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:09.568
'cause he switched from
employee to contractor.

00:03:09.568 --> 00:03:13.231
So that incredible outcome
couldn't have happened

00:03:13.231 --> 00:03:15.372
if he hadn't thought
about the other person.

00:03:15.372 --> 00:03:19.615
- So I was um, taking off
my um, man thong and um,

00:03:19.615 --> 00:03:22.981
um, uh, and I was like oh my God,

00:03:22.981 --> 00:03:25.349
and you were like oh
my God, he has a wife?

00:03:25.349 --> 00:03:26.182
Like.

00:03:26.182 --> 00:03:28.136
Yo dawg, I'm so pissed right now,

00:03:28.136 --> 00:03:29.251
you'll never believe what happened.

00:03:29.251 --> 00:03:32.576
I was over there, ordering the
delicious ass McRib sandwich,

00:03:32.576 --> 00:03:33.789
you know what I'm sayin'?

00:03:33.789 --> 00:03:35.461
And they forgot the
mother freakin' pickle!

00:03:35.461 --> 00:03:36.483
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:03:36.483 --> 00:03:38.421
Yes, I do know what you're saying,

00:03:38.421 --> 00:03:41.085
that the McRib sandwich
is a culinary mystery meat

00:03:41.085 --> 00:03:44.370
that needs pickle to
enhance it's deliciousness.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.233
I also know that using filler words

00:03:46.233 --> 00:03:48.996
makes you sound unconfident,
unsure of yourself,

00:03:48.996 --> 00:03:53.099
unprofessional, and dare
I say dumb as a stump?

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:54.736
So what about you?

00:03:54.736 --> 00:03:56.396
Do you use filler words?

00:03:56.396 --> 00:03:57.650
And if so, what is it?

00:03:57.650 --> 00:03:59.594
If you're not sure, ask
your friends or family,

00:03:59.594 --> 00:04:02.392
because if you don't
know, I'm sure they will.

00:04:02.392 --> 00:04:04.545
Because that's exactly how I found out

00:04:04.545 --> 00:04:07.302
that I use the word um all the time.

00:04:07.302 --> 00:04:09.223
Tracy, one day about six years ago,

00:04:09.223 --> 00:04:10.273
came up and was like,

00:04:10.273 --> 00:04:11.753
"You say um all the time."

00:04:11.753 --> 00:04:13.564
I was like, "I do?"

00:04:13.564 --> 00:04:15.364
I told her to start pointing it out.

00:04:15.364 --> 00:04:18.622
Every time she noticed or
heard me use the word um,

00:04:18.622 --> 00:04:20.455
she would say, "Ummm."

00:04:21.551 --> 00:04:23.541
And that was my cue to
start paying attention,

00:04:23.541 --> 00:04:25.283
but something remarkable happened.

00:04:25.283 --> 00:04:27.970
After a little while of
being ultra aware of myself,

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:29.862
I would actually start correcting myself.

00:04:29.862 --> 00:04:32.008
When I'd say um, I'd be like, "Uh um."

00:04:32.008 --> 00:04:33.987
It was really awkward and uncomfortable

00:04:33.987 --> 00:04:36.582
when I was having a
conversation with somebody else.

00:04:36.582 --> 00:04:37.946
And eventually I stopped.

00:04:37.946 --> 00:04:41.144
It took hard work, being hyper
aware of what you're saying,

00:04:41.144 --> 00:04:42.554
and a little bit paranoid,

00:04:42.554 --> 00:04:45.201
but eventually I kicked
it, and so can you.

00:04:45.201 --> 00:04:47.865
And a great tip I would give
to you is when you're talking

00:04:47.865 --> 00:04:49.960
and you feel it coming on, just

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:51.161
(pause)

00:04:51.161 --> 00:04:52.984
pause, and pick up where you left off.

00:04:52.984 --> 00:04:54.406
And eventually you will be able

00:04:54.406 --> 00:04:56.814
to kick the filler word habit.

00:04:56.814 --> 00:04:58.277
- A bit of a warning,

00:04:58.277 --> 00:05:01.301
what I'm about to tell
you to do is terrifying

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:04.626
and most of use spend our
entire lives trying to avoid it.

00:05:04.626 --> 00:05:08.793
But, like a pap smear, it's
over fast, and it really works.

00:05:10.587 --> 00:05:13.920
(gentle breeze blowing)

00:05:14.876 --> 00:05:16.310
I just did it!

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:17.477
Did you catch it?

00:05:17.477 --> 00:05:19.618
If not, and you missed
it, here it goes again.

00:05:19.618 --> 00:05:22.009
(gentle breeze blowing)

00:05:22.009 --> 00:05:23.931
- [Voiceover] Awkward.

00:05:23.931 --> 00:05:25.428
- That was a live demonstration

00:05:25.428 --> 00:05:29.595
of the three second IAP: the
Intentional Awkward Pause.

00:05:30.599 --> 00:05:32.468
- [Voiceover] Oh my
God, this is so awkward.

00:05:32.468 --> 00:05:33.774
- Now why on God's green earth

00:05:33.774 --> 00:05:35.301
would you ever want to do that?

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:36.134
I'll tell you.

00:05:36.134 --> 00:05:38.690
Because being a really,
really good listener

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:41.314
is one of the most underrated
secrets to success.

00:05:41.314 --> 00:05:43.281
It's crucial if you want
to be more charismatic,

00:05:43.281 --> 00:05:45.986
more persuasive, and a better negotiator.

00:05:45.986 --> 00:05:48.220
Plus, really listening to someone

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.449
and having them feel truly heard

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:52.939
is one of the biggest
gifts you could ever give.

00:05:52.939 --> 00:05:54.930
The problem is most of
us suck at listening

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.329
and we don't even know it.

00:05:56.329 --> 00:05:59.484
We're always interrupting
others with our brilliant ideas

00:05:59.484 --> 00:06:01.662
and our feedback and
most often it's the way--

00:06:01.662 --> 00:06:03.166
- Oh my God, that is so true,

00:06:03.166 --> 00:06:05.499
I am always interrupting people.

00:06:05.499 --> 00:06:06.921
(quirky music)

00:06:06.921 --> 00:06:11.036
- As I was saying, it's often
the way that we suck the most.

00:06:11.036 --> 00:06:13.224
Now I'm probably one of the
worst offenders out there,

00:06:13.224 --> 00:06:15.586
but I am consistently working
on this and I have to say

00:06:15.586 --> 00:06:17.739
the IAP is a life saver.

00:06:17.739 --> 00:06:20.601
Not so long ago I used
it with my stepson, Zane,

00:06:20.601 --> 00:06:23.089
and had an unbelievable result.

00:06:23.089 --> 00:06:26.339
So Zane was home on
Christmas break from college

00:06:26.339 --> 00:06:28.678
and he invited me to go for cappuccinos.

00:06:28.678 --> 00:06:31.076
A little back story here,

00:06:31.076 --> 00:06:33.745
in the 10 years plus that we've
been in each other's lives,

00:06:33.745 --> 00:06:36.525
he had never really
asked me for life advice.

00:06:36.525 --> 00:06:38.831
So we get to this place, Cafe Minerva,

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:40.050
and we're hanging out,

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.638
and Zane starts to tell
me all about his life

00:06:42.638 --> 00:06:44.455
and what he might want to do with it.

00:06:44.455 --> 00:06:46.498
He's very, very involved in music,

00:06:46.498 --> 00:06:48.837
he's involved in acting and
all these creative things,

00:06:48.837 --> 00:06:51.286
so basically he's asking
me for the kind of advice

00:06:51.286 --> 00:06:54.009
that I give for a living.

00:06:54.009 --> 00:06:56.342
So you could imagine, every
single part of me just wanted

00:06:56.342 --> 00:06:58.750
to jump in and tell him what to do

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:00.909
and tell him what I
think and yada yada yada.

00:07:00.909 --> 00:07:03.231
But I practiced the IAP,

00:07:03.231 --> 00:07:05.712
and I just sat there and I zipped it.

00:07:05.712 --> 00:07:07.442
Now, every time I did this,

00:07:07.442 --> 00:07:10.263
and I just shut up, it was amazing,

00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:13.136
'cause he would reveal
more and more insights,

00:07:13.136 --> 00:07:15.086
he'd find new things to talk about,

00:07:15.086 --> 00:07:17.193
he'd find his own insights
that were way better

00:07:17.193 --> 00:07:18.853
than anything I could have told him,

00:07:18.853 --> 00:07:20.472
and it was really, really amazing.

00:07:20.472 --> 00:07:21.523
Now I have to be honest,

00:07:21.523 --> 00:07:25.086
my body was literally
uncomfortable being silent,

00:07:25.086 --> 00:07:27.147
so every time he would come
to the end of a sentence,

00:07:27.147 --> 00:07:28.081
I would sit

00:07:28.081 --> 00:07:29.277
(gentle breeze blowing)

00:07:29.277 --> 00:07:30.635
and see if there was more.

00:07:30.635 --> 00:07:32.730
And it was absolutely awesome.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:34.251
By the end of three hours,

00:07:34.251 --> 00:07:36.932
we had the most beautiful conversation,

00:07:36.932 --> 00:07:40.798
and we left that cafe
feeling more connected

00:07:40.798 --> 00:07:44.030
and more intimate than
I think we've ever felt.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.149
So, just a little story to show

00:07:46.149 --> 00:07:48.639
that it really, really works.

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:51.256
- Another way to become
a great conversationalist

00:07:51.256 --> 00:07:54.006
is to question for clarification.

00:07:55.325 --> 00:07:56.892
Never assume that you understand

00:07:56.892 --> 00:08:00.438
what the other person is
saying or trying to say.

00:08:00.438 --> 00:08:02.394
Instead, if you have any doubt at all,

00:08:02.394 --> 00:08:04.954
ask, "How do you mean?"

00:08:04.954 --> 00:08:07.972
Or, "How do you mean exactly?"

00:08:07.972 --> 00:08:10.706
And then just pause, and wait.

00:08:10.706 --> 00:08:14.358
This is the most powerful
question I've ever learned

00:08:14.358 --> 00:08:18.409
for guiding and
controlling a conversation.

00:08:18.409 --> 00:08:22.554
It's almost impossible not
to answer this question.

00:08:22.554 --> 00:08:24.823
When you ask, "How do you mean?"

00:08:24.823 --> 00:08:28.253
the other person cannot
stop himself or herself

00:08:28.253 --> 00:08:31.537
from answering more extensively.

00:08:31.537 --> 00:08:34.671
You can then follow up with
other open ended questions

00:08:34.671 --> 00:08:37.713
and keep the conversation rolling along.

00:08:37.713 --> 00:08:40.185
- Begin with the end in mind.

00:08:40.185 --> 00:08:44.764
How do you want them to feel
at the end of the conversation?

00:08:44.764 --> 00:08:49.158
How do you want to feel at
the end of the conversation?

00:08:49.158 --> 00:08:50.766
What would you love them to think,

00:08:50.766 --> 00:08:52.994
believe, or do at the end,

00:08:52.994 --> 00:08:56.285
and what do you want to think,
believe, or do at the end?

00:08:56.285 --> 00:08:57.847
Always begin with that.

00:08:57.847 --> 00:08:59.385
People forget this all the time.

00:08:59.385 --> 00:09:00.952
They just have a spark of emotion

00:09:00.952 --> 00:09:02.182
or something they have to deliver,

00:09:02.182 --> 00:09:05.177
and they just deliver it
without knowing where to go

00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:06.640
and if you don't know where to go

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:10.099
you end up in 50 different
sort of open alleys

00:09:10.099 --> 00:09:12.188
of conversation, you open all
these loops of conversation

00:09:12.188 --> 00:09:15.299
that don't get closed,
people get confused,

00:09:15.299 --> 00:09:16.628
or you end up starting a fight

00:09:16.628 --> 00:09:19.194
that you didn't even know you were having,

00:09:19.194 --> 00:09:22.090
'cause you just weren't clear
on where you wanted to go.

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:24.052
It doesn't mean that you'll
always get there exactly,

00:09:24.052 --> 00:09:26.251
'cause again, humility says
you're going to learn together,

00:09:26.251 --> 00:09:30.668
but at least you'll have a
sense of what feeling you want.

00:09:30.668 --> 00:09:34.261
Do you want her to trust you
at the end of the conversation?

00:09:34.261 --> 00:09:36.850
Do you want him to know how you felt

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:38.585
and that's all you're after?

00:09:38.585 --> 00:09:41.543
What is it you really want at
the end of the conversation?

00:09:41.543 --> 00:09:43.550
(swooping music)

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:45.775
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:09:45.775 --> 00:09:47.632
I'd love to know what did
you think of this video,

00:09:47.632 --> 00:09:50.320
what was the rule that most applies to you

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:52.833
that you're going to
immediately implement somehow

00:09:52.833 --> 00:09:54.655
into your life or your business.

00:09:54.655 --> 00:09:56.373
Please let me know, I'd
love to hear from you,

00:09:56.373 --> 00:09:57.894
leave it down in the comments below.

00:09:57.894 --> 00:10:00.459
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Anthony Avina.

00:10:00.459 --> 00:10:02.462
Anthony, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:10:02.462 --> 00:10:05.346
Your One Word, and doing
the video review of it.

00:10:05.346 --> 00:10:07.192
I really appreciate the support, man,

00:10:07.192 --> 00:10:09.542
and I'm so glad that you enjoyed the book.

00:10:09.542 --> 00:10:11.812
- Today I'm going to be reviewing
a book for you guys called,

00:10:11.812 --> 00:10:14.702
Your One Word, by Evan Carmichael.

00:10:14.702 --> 00:10:16.269
- So thank you guys again for watching,

00:10:16.269 --> 00:10:18.160
I hope you have an amazing day.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:19.936
I believe in you and hope you continue

00:10:19.936 --> 00:10:23.808
to believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is.

00:10:23.808 --> 00:10:26.274
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:10:26.274 --> 00:10:29.024
(swooping music)

00:10:32.055 --> 00:10:34.667
- How you do communicate authentically?

00:10:34.667 --> 00:10:36.518
How do you figure out what to say

00:10:36.518 --> 00:10:39.229
and what not to say in
a way that's authentic?

00:10:39.229 --> 00:10:41.846
And what Fred says, and I
really believe this is true,

00:10:41.846 --> 00:10:44.702
is it starts from the
fundamental understanding

00:10:44.702 --> 00:10:46.577
that there is no truth.

00:10:46.577 --> 00:10:47.708
There's my truth, there's your truth,

00:10:47.708 --> 00:10:49.937
and everything is subjective.

00:10:49.937 --> 00:10:51.577
And so if you always
start from the position

00:10:51.577 --> 00:10:53.207
of this is what I believe,

00:10:53.207 --> 00:10:54.751
I don't expect you to believe it,

00:10:54.751 --> 00:10:55.982
I don't think you have to believe it,

00:10:55.982 --> 00:10:57.764
I'm not saying it's true,

00:10:57.764 --> 00:11:00.462
you can actually always
communicate authentically.

00:11:00.462 --> 00:11:01.577
'Cause if you walk in the room,

00:11:01.577 --> 00:11:03.382
and this gets worse as
you get more senior,

00:11:03.382 --> 00:11:05.164
here's the answer,

00:11:05.164 --> 00:11:07.758
you're not giving anyone else
any room to say anything.

00:11:07.758 --> 00:11:09.552
And if you walk in the room and say,

00:11:09.552 --> 00:11:14.009
"I believe this for this
reason, what do you believe?"

00:11:14.009 --> 00:11:17.724
If you share your truth in that language,

00:11:17.724 --> 00:11:20.022
you give people room to authenticate,

00:11:20.022 --> 00:11:22.106
to communicate authentically.

00:11:22.106 --> 00:11:23.725
And that is hugely important

00:11:23.725 --> 00:11:25.826
to these relationships at any stage.

00:11:25.826 --> 00:11:30.167
- Paraphrase the speaker's
words in your own words.

00:11:30.167 --> 00:11:32.756
So after you've nodded and
smiled, you can then say,

00:11:32.756 --> 00:11:35.136
"Well let me see if I've got this right,"

00:11:35.136 --> 00:11:37.660
or "Let me see if I
understand you exactly."

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:40.452
What you're saying is this,

00:11:40.452 --> 00:11:43.638
and then you repeat it
back in your own words.

00:11:43.638 --> 00:11:46.001
By paraphrasing the speaker's words,

00:11:46.001 --> 00:11:48.525
you demonstrate in no uncertain terms

00:11:48.525 --> 00:11:50.980
that you are genuinely paying attention

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:53.662
and making every effort to understand

00:11:53.662 --> 00:11:56.291
his or her thoughts or feelings.

00:11:56.291 --> 00:11:58.177
And the wonderful thing is,

00:11:58.177 --> 00:12:00.522
when you practice effective listening,

00:12:00.522 --> 00:12:04.359
other people will begin
to find you fascinating.

00:12:04.359 --> 00:12:05.926
They will want to be around you.

00:12:05.926 --> 00:12:08.845
They will feel relaxed and
happy in your presence,

00:12:08.845 --> 00:12:11.393
because when you listen to other people

00:12:11.393 --> 00:12:13.813
you make them feel important.

00:12:13.813 --> 00:12:18.219
- Nelson Mandela is a
particularly special case study

00:12:18.219 --> 00:12:20.331
in the leadership world,

00:12:20.331 --> 00:12:24.213
because he is universally
regarded as a great leader.

00:12:24.213 --> 00:12:26.401
You can take other personalities,

00:12:26.401 --> 00:12:27.759
and depending on the nation you go to,

00:12:27.759 --> 00:12:30.040
we have different opinions
of other personalities,

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:32.147
but Nelson Mandela across the world

00:12:32.147 --> 00:12:35.386
is universally regarded as a great leader.

00:12:35.386 --> 00:12:39.304
He was actually the son of a tribal chief

00:12:39.304 --> 00:12:41.480
and he was asked one day,

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:45.032
"How did you learn to be a great leader?"

00:12:45.032 --> 00:12:48.195
And he responded that he
would go with his father

00:12:48.195 --> 00:12:52.154
to tribal meetings and
he remembers two things

00:12:52.154 --> 00:12:54.522
when his father would
meet with other elders.

00:12:54.522 --> 00:12:57.772
One, they would always sit in a circle.

00:12:59.165 --> 00:13:03.248
And two, his father was
always the last to speak.

00:13:05.166 --> 00:13:06.588
You will be told your whole life

00:13:06.588 --> 00:13:08.486
that you need to learn to listen,

00:13:08.486 --> 00:13:12.386
I would say that you need to
learn to be the last to speak.

00:13:12.386 --> 00:13:14.662
I see it in board rooms
every day of the week.

00:13:14.662 --> 00:13:16.809
Even people who consider
themselves good leaders,

00:13:16.809 --> 00:13:18.365
who may actually be decent leaders,

00:13:18.365 --> 00:13:20.088
will walk into a room and say,

00:13:20.088 --> 00:13:21.522
"Here's the problem, here's what I think,

00:13:21.522 --> 00:13:22.787
"but I'm interested in your opinion,

00:13:22.787 --> 00:13:23.994
"let's go around the room."

00:13:23.994 --> 00:13:25.637
It's too late.

00:13:25.637 --> 00:13:28.266
The skill to hold your
opinions to yourself

00:13:28.266 --> 00:13:30.977
until everyone has spoken does two things.

00:13:30.977 --> 00:13:33.513
One, it gives everyone else the feeling

00:13:33.513 --> 00:13:35.939
that they have been heard.

00:13:35.939 --> 00:13:37.831
It gives everyone else the ability

00:13:37.831 --> 00:13:40.838
to feel that they have contributed.

00:13:40.838 --> 00:13:42.974
And two, you get the benefits of hearing

00:13:42.974 --> 00:13:46.578
what everybody has to think
before you render your opinion.

00:13:46.578 --> 00:13:49.695
The skill is really to keep
your opinions to yourself.

00:13:49.695 --> 00:13:53.380
If you agree with somebody, don't nod yes.

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:56.903
If you disagree with
somebody, don't nod no.

00:13:56.903 --> 00:13:58.459
Simply sit there, take it all in,

00:13:58.459 --> 00:14:01.355
and the only thing you're
allowed to do is ask questions

00:14:01.355 --> 00:14:04.141
so you can understand what they mean

00:14:04.141 --> 00:14:06.160
and why they have they
opinion that they have.

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:09.781
You must understand from
where they are speaking,

00:14:09.781 --> 00:14:12.817
why they have the opinion they have,

00:14:12.817 --> 00:14:15.423
not just what they are saying.

00:14:15.423 --> 00:14:18.673
And at the end, you will get your turn.

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:21.603
It sounds easy, it's not.

00:14:22.728 --> 00:14:24.980
Practice being the last to speak.

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.148
That's what Nelson Mandela did.

00:14:27.148 --> 00:14:27.981
(swooping music)

